{
  "dom_id": "step-q158364",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Compute $P(2<X\\le 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{21}{x}^{2},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{21}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{63}{x}^{3}]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{63}(27-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
